End with a short summary (under 200 words): the files you wrote, which events and sounds are implemented, anything you
could not do, and any assumption you made about `from`/`to` in `heroStep`, `pos` in `beacon`, or the decor `worldPos()`
return type. Do not paste code into the summary.
### 7.7 FX (`js/fx.js`) and UI (`js/ui.js`)

- `FX.setup(Game)`:
  - Particles: ≤ 1500 live, ≤ 8 draw calls, pooled.
  - Synthesized WebAudio: no files, started on the first gesture, respecting `Game.save.sound` and `Game.save.music`.
  - `FX.sound(name)` for UI clicks.
  - Under `prefers-reduced-motion`, use half the particles and no camera motion.
- `UI.setup(Game)`:
  - DOM only, inside `Game.ui`.
  - Screens: title, spires map (level select), HUD (pause, restart, hint, glimmers), intro card, pause, settings,
    level-complete card, ending.
  - Touch-friendly (≥ 44 px targets), keyboard-usable and responsive (portrait and landscape).

## 8. Events (`Game.bus`)

All positions in event payloads are world coordinates as `[x, y, z]` arrays.

| event | data | when |
|---|---|---|
| `title` | `{}` | title screen shown |
| `levelStart` | `{ index, level }` | level built, intro card time |
| `tap` | `{ x, y, tile, reachable, pos }` | a walk tap (x, y in CSS px; tile = walkable id or null; pos = its world stand point or null) |
| `path` | `{ points: [[x,y,z]...] }` | a walk begins (world stand points) |
| `heroStep` | `{ from, to, k, at }` | each tile transition: from/to are walkable ids, k ≠ 0 means an impossible join, at = world [x,y,z] of the junction on the `from` side |
| `heroStop` | `{ tile }` | the walk ended |
| `mechGrab` | `{ id, kind }` | a handle was grabbed |
| `mechMove` | `{ id, value, delta }` | every frame while a mechanism moves |
| `mechTick` | `{ id, pos }` | every 15° (rotor) or 0.25 units (slider) passed (pos = handle world position) |
| `mechSnap` | `{ id, value, pos }` | settled on an allowed value (pos = handle world position) |
| `mechLocked` | `{ id }` | the move was refused (Wick on an x/z rotor) |
| `plate` | `{ tile, group, pos, target }` | a plate was pressed (pos = plate world position, target = world centre of the group it moves) |
| `glimmer` | `{ tile, count, total, pos }` | a glimmer was collected (pos = its world position) |
| `goal` | `{ index }` | Wick reached the goal |
| `beacon` | `{ index, pos }` | the beacon ignites: the engine has just called the beacon decor's `ignite()`; FX runs the cascade (`setLit` on the other decor) and the particles. pos = world [x,y,z] of the beacon flame |
| `levelComplete` | `{ index, glimmers, total, next }` | show the complete card |
| `restart` | `{ index }` | the level restarted |
| `pause` | `{ paused }` | the game was paused or resumed |
| `quality` | `{ level }` | the quality tier changed |

**P1-8. levels: plates do not telegraph what they move.** After a plate fires on L3, L5, L8, L9 or L10, the moving part is often off in another corner.

- **levels:** run a `pipe` or `chain` decor along the base from each plate toward its target group.
- **fx:** on the `plate` event, send a 0.6 s spark along a straight screen-space line from the plate's `pos` to the target group's centre (it is in the `plate` payload's `group`).

**P1-9. levels + decor: compositions are scattered chunks, not spires.**

- **Spire-shaped:** L4, L7 and the title read as spires.
- **Not spire-shaped:**
  - L3, L5, L6, L9 and L10 are 2–3 separate slabs at different heights.
  - L2 is one flat slab.
- **Fix, without touching the geometry:**
  - (a) Give every level a central **spine**: a tall non-walkable shaft that rises behind the beacon.
  - (b) Use one shared **keel** under the whole footprint. Today the underside is a tiny `taper` plus one copper finial; the key art has stepped corbel rings, a gear cluster and a long brass pendant.
  - (c) Visually tie floating islands to the spine with **decor struts and arches**. Decor never takes part in the raycast, so struts cannot break illusions.
- **New decor types:** `keel` (params `w`, `d`, `depth`) and `strut` (`from`, `to`).

### Visual quality

**P1-1. world: exposure, fill and sky per theme.** The key art is deep teal/violet over saturated orange; ours is pale cyan-pink (dusk) and white-peach (dawn). The fill is too strong: hemisphere 0.85–1.0 plus a PMREM sky environment on every block, against a key light of only 1.4. As a result every face has nearly the same brightness, and cast shadows barely show (`l1-intro-p.png`, `l9-p.png`). Starting values in `DATA`:

| Theme | Exposure | Key light | Hemisphere | Other |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| dusk | 0.95 | 2.1 | 0.45 | Sky gain about −20% at the top, so the zenith reads `#1d1b45`. |
| night | 0.78 | 0.8 (moon) | 0.4 | Turn `rays` and `glare` off (night currently shows sun rays, `L4-p.png`). Zenith navy `#0d1030`. The lantern cascade should be the brightest thing on screen. |
| aurora | 0.9 | – | 0.5 | Cloud sea darker (mint is too bright and saturated). |
| dawn | 0.92 | 2.2, low from the left | 0.5 | Cloud sea at peach mid-tone, not white. Lower the glare. |

- Keep all mist screen-space.
- Compare every change side by side with `art-src/style/style-1.png` at 390×844.

**P1-2. blocks: the material palette lacks value separation.**

| Material | Current | Fix |
|---|---|---|
